In LaTeX, you can left-align the caption of a table without using the `caption` package by redefining the `\@makecaption` command for tables. Here's how you can do it:

### Method 1: Using `\captionsetup` (if you're not strictly avoiding `caption` package)
If you're open to using a minimal part of the `caption` package (but not loading the full package), you can use:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[singlelinecheck=off]{caption} % Only this option, no other caption features
\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
\captionsetup{justification=raggedright,singlelinecheck=false}
\caption{This is a left-aligned table caption without using the full caption package.}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

### Method 2: Redefining `\@makecaption` (no `caption` package)
If you want to avoid the `caption` package entirely, you can redefine the `\@makecaption` command for tables:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

% Redefine \@makecaption for tables to left-align captions
\makeatletter
\long\def\@makecaption#1#2{%
  \vskip\abovecaptionskip
  \sbox\@tempboxa{#1: #2}%
  \ifdim \wd\@tempboxa >\hsize
    #1: #2\par
  \else
    \global \@minipagefalse
    \hbox to\hsize{\hfil\box\@tempboxa\hfil}% Centered by default, change to \hbox to\hsize{\box\@tempboxa} for left-aligned
  \fi
  \vskip\belowcaptionskip}
\makeatother

% For left-aligned table captions only, add this:
\makeatletter
\long\def\@maketablecaption#1#2{%
  \vskip\abovecaptionskip
  \sbox\@tempboxa{#1: #2}%
  \ifdim \wd\@tempboxa >\hsize
    #1: #2\par
  \else
    \global \@minipagefalse
    \hbox to\hsize{\box\@tempboxa}% Left-aligned
  \fi
  \vskip\belowcaptionskip}
\makeatother

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
\makeatletter
\let\@makecaption\@maketablecaption % Use the left-aligned version for this table
\makeatother
\caption{This is a left-aligned table caption without the caption package.}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

% Other tables will use the default centered caption
\begin{table}[h]
\caption{This table caption is centered (default).}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
E & F \\
G & H \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

### Method 3: Using `\raggedright` inside the caption
A simpler (but less elegant) way is to use `\raggedright` inside the caption:

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
\caption{\raggedright This is a left-aligned table caption.}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
```

### Best Approach:
- If you want **all table captions left-aligned**, use **Method 2** (redefining `\@makecaption`).
- If you want **only some captions left-aligned**, use **Method 3** (`\raggedright` inside `\caption`).
- If you're okay with a minimal part of the `caption` package, **Method 1** is the cleanest.

